abstract: >
  Empirical study of QLoRA continued pretraining of a 7B code model
  (Qwen 2.5 Coder 7B Instruct) on 18,013 CTF (Capture-the-Flag) writeup
  chunks scraped from CTFtime.org. The fine-tuned model
  (masafee-ctf-7b) is evaluated on the CyberMetric-500 cybersecurity
  knowledge benchmark and a 30-challenge subset of the NYU CTF Bench
  alongside the base model and Cisco's Foundation-Sec-8B-Instruct.
  Findings: (1) continued pretraining on writeup text produces
  measurable style transfer (directness, CTF-specific terminology)
  but no measurable capability gain on knowledge MCQs or single-shot
  CTF problem solving; (2) style overfitting can actively impede
  flag-answer extraction; (3) the writeup-trained model exhibits an
  11x lower hedging-language rate than the SOC-tuned Foundation-Sec-8B,
  reflecting domain-specific training-data priors rather than a
  quality difference. All training was performed on a single
  consumer GPU (RTX 3060 12GB) in 12 hours 17 minutes.
